4:**VERDICT: SHIP WITH CHANGES — the chain CLOSES on this pass.** M1 is L4, not L2, but only with the bound stated below; M19 is the boundary's own class, unmeasured, and owes one build tonight plus a filed gate-side close. Every change is contract wording (comment-only; prove code identity by the preprocessed md5 as before), so no further panel pass is owed; Astra confirms after 07:01. **Count: 0 of the main loop's dispositions overturned, 1 refined (M19), M15's seat position rejected, 17 upheld.**
8:## Q1 — M1..M19
22:- **M16 UPHOLD, and it changes the lane's spec:** with the report in place the lane is no longer for resets; it closes (i) probe/real keys for the rows its mutants fail and (ii) skip-forgery for the groups its mutants sit in. So: one capacity row through `row_cmp()` AND one ring row through `chk()`, each on its own device mutant. A lane is a sample, and the contract should say so.
24:- **M18 UPHOLD the naming, not the "reasoned red"** — four cells, one build; measure them beside M19 tonight.
25:- **M19 REFINE.** It is not (C) as worded ("rows not run") and not the (B) residual; it is the boundary's general form — the healthy observables forged AFTER the rows ran. (C) must be generalised to "observables forged: rows skipped with their labels printed, or the log rewritten by the process". It is WALKED, not measured: measure it (one build; the shell redirect makes stdout a seekable file, so `fseek` should work — but a claim in a contract is a hypothesis until built). And file the gate-side close nobody named: read the log through a pipe (`"$WDCBIN" 2>&1 | cat > "$WDCLOG"`, or `tee`) — an unseekable stdout cannot be rewritten, one gate line, its own negative control (M19's construction must go red under it). Until then (B)'s "hence" sentence is scoped as below, and the shape is named as measured or as walked, whichever the build says.
27:## Q2 — L4 or L2, and why the chain closes
29:L4 for M1 and M19; the chain closes. The stop rule's L2 clause was written for a defect in the MECHANISM — something the report or the probes were supposed to catch and do not. Five passes have now found exactly two residual classes, and both are theorems, not gaps: (i) a comparator whose compiled behaviour on real calls differs from its behaviour on the self-check's calls (no finite probe set closes it; M1, M2, the keys, the sum form, the feed edit are its members, whether written in the comparator's text or through the preprocessor), and (ii) the healthy observables reproduced (rows skipped with forged output; the log rewritten; counters written). The report closed everything that is NOT in (i) or (ii) — every trailer rewrite, every reset, every move or deferral with the rows still running — and that is measured red twice, with the real `lib.sh` and the real `selfmutant.py`. A green shape that lands in (i) or (ii) is a contract fact by construction, however novel its lever, because the two classes are defined by what an in-file check CAN observe, not by a list of edits. That is what makes this pass terminal and the next escape a clause-(e) question. The wording fixes are comments only; Astra reviews the final text.
31:## Q3 — the text
35:> WHAT THIS FILE'S CHECKS CAN AND CANNOT SEE.  Every check here reads state this file writes (the tallies, the call counts, `rows', sc_g) or output this file prints, so an edit that reproduces a healthy run's observables -- by skipping rows and printing what they would have printed, by writing what a check reads, or by rewriting the log after the rows have run -- is invisible to all of them.  That boundary is a fact about in-file checks, not a gap in this block.  Two things cross it: a gate-side run on a device that fails a covered row -- one capacity row through row_cmp() AND one ring row through chk(), each on its own device mutant, because a skip of one group leaves the other group's lane green, and a lane closes only the rows its mutants fail (filed: `wdcidlane') -- and the log reaching the gate through a pipe rather than a seekable file, which no exit handler can rewrite (filed with it).  Inside the boundary:
47:> (C) OBSERVABLES FORGED -- a row that does not run cannot report, and a log rewritten after the rows have run reports nothing.  Skipping the ring rows while printing their gate-pinned labels and adding 24 to `rows' and chk_calls is one insertion and green (measured); an exit handler that seeks a file-redirected stdout to 0 and writes a healthy log over the real one is one insertion and [green -- measured / walked, not measured] (M19, #551f's pass).  The identity row's 128 and the LAST run's call counts raise the cost of a skip; nothing in this file raises the cost of a rewrite.  That is the boundary's own class; its close is `wdcidlane' and the pipe.
51:> "Covered" means the COMPARATOR is live, not that a row is.  What this file's checks can see stops at what the file itself writes.  CAN keep this gate green -- measured on #551's passes, disclosed, not chased: any difference between a comparator's behaviour on the self-check's calls and on real calls (a keyed or probe-agreeing test, feeding it its own want, or its failure report kept from this gate's count check while the tallies are hidden -- one edit or two, in the comparator's text or through a file-scope macro); an edit of the self-check itself; and the observables forged (covered rows skipped with their labels and counts printed, or the log rewritten by the process after the rows ran).  Anything else -- a trailer rewrite, a reset of either or both tallies anywhere, rows moved or deferred with their counts forged -- is caught: a covered failure prints a line this gate's `[0-9]+ failures' check reads at the moment it fails.  The file covers 24 comparisons and reports all 32 comparator failures.  The gate-side close is a lane whose device mutants fail one capacity row and one ring row, and the log read through a pipe (filed: `wdcidlane').  #551, by owner ruling 2026-09-25 ("24/128 now, file the rest"); the rest is `wdcidcomparators'.
53:## Q4 — wrong acceptances, and what all of us missed
55:No acceptance reverses its finding. Two are incomplete: M19 (an unmeasured shape treated as settled, (C) not generalised, no close filed) and M1 (right ruling, missing the bound that stops L4 from reading as a definition shift). Missed by everyone including me: (1) the one-line gate-side close for the log rewrite — a pipe makes stdout unseekable, and it needs its own negative control; (2) with the report in place the lane's job changed, and "the FIRST covered row" — my own instruction from two passes ago — was carried forward without re-deriving what it was for; M16 caught the skip half, and the keyed half (a lane closes only the rows its mutants fail) is still unsaid; (3) `(printf)(...)` — a parenthesised call is not a macro invocation — would kill M1's exact lever for one token; it is a cost-raiser, not a closure, since the class has other levers, so it is optional and must not be described as a close; (4) M18's four cells are cheap and should be measured, not reasoned, in the same build as M19; (5) the round record should carry the closing sentence itself — five passes, two residual classes, both theorems — so the next reader knows why the chain stopped and classifies the next escape against the contract instead of reopening it.

